+
+# Decision: How concurrent visit edits become a current record
+
+## Context
+
+Field workers edit visits on tablets that can be offline for 48 hours, and they sometimes correct the same visit independently after comparing field notes. The record store therefore receives edits that were made from the same starting state without knowledge of each other, days apart, in an order that says nothing about which worker is right.
+
+The record store keeps every original submission whatever policy it follows, so no option here loses data. What the policy decides is what the server does *next*: which state of the visit counts as current, how much of that a coordinator has to look at, and what can reach the partner's Friday CSV. Tablet-sync and review-export are both sized around the answer: it fixes what a tablet must store and send per edit, what a coordinator's review work looks like, and which visits an export may include.
+
+If the policy is wrong in one direction, a scientifically incoherent visit reaches the partner looking reviewed. If it is wrong in the other, two coordinators cannot clear review work before Friday and good visits are withheld from the export.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+Weighted criteria:
+
+- **Coherence of a visit's scientific content — major.** A visit's fields are not independent: a species identification and its cover estimate are one observation, and the server cannot learn every such relationship from field names. Importance derives from the tenet *Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable* and the non-goal *automated botanical validation or inference of scientific intent*. A policy that produces a visit no worker ever saw is inferring intent.
+- **Coordinator review workload — major.** Two coordinators resolve every question before a weekly export, across about 6,000 visits a season. Importance derives from the tenet *Prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator* and the goal *Make reviewable problems visible before the Friday export*.
+- **Export readiness — moderate.** A visit awaiting review is absent from the Friday CSV. Related to workload but not the same: it measures what the partner does not receive, even when the queue is eventually cleared.
+- **Upkeep of the policy itself — moderate.** Rules that must change when the visit's fields change need someone to own them. Derives from the same no-systems-administrator tenet.
+- **Explainability of an exported value — moderate.** A coordinator or the partner asks why a value is what it is. Derives from the inspectable-path tenet.
+- **Cost of changing policy later — moderate.** One season of real conflict data will say more about review volume than any estimate made beforehand, so the ability to move to the other policy without re-collecting or migrating submissions matters.
+- **Tablet storage and submission size — minor.** At 6,000 visits a season on 12 tablets, neither option strains storage or bandwidth; the difference is in how much state the tablet must keep correct.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-visit revisions
+
+Every edit is a complete copy of the visit and names the revision it was edited from. An edit built on the visit's current revision replaces it. Edits that do not build on one another become competing complete revisions, and the visit has no current revision until a coordinator resolves them. The server never combines fields from different submissions; the revision is the smallest unit it compares, supersedes, or sends to review.
+
+Demonstration: worker A corrects an observer's name; worker B, from the same starting revision, adds a missing species. Both arrive. The visit is unresolved with two competing revisions, and a coordinator resolves it even though the edits do not touch the same field.
+
+- Coherence — **strong.** Every state the server treats as current is a visit some person submitted or a coordinator decided on, whole.
+- Review workload — **weak.** Every pair of independent edits is review work, including edits to unrelated fields that a person would combine at a glance.
+- Export readiness — **weak.** Each such visit is withheld from the CSV until resolved, so visits with trivially compatible edits can miss a Friday.
+- Upkeep — **strong.** The policy has one rule, based on which revision an edit names as its base. Adding a field to a visit changes nothing in it.
+- Explainability — **strong.** An exported visit is one revision, traceable through base links and resolution records to the submissions and coordinator behind it.
+- Cost of changing later — **strong.** Complete revisions and their bases are exactly the inputs a three-way merge takes. A merge could be introduced later over the stored history, or first as a suggestion shown to the coordinator, with no change to what tablets submitted in the past.
+- Tablet storage — **strong.** One base revision ID per local copy.
+
+Summary: trades coordinator time and export timeliness for never producing a visit nobody wrote.
+
+### Field-aware three-way merge
+
+Every edit supplies the state it started from and the changes made. When two edits share a starting state, the server compares each against it: changes to different fields are combined automatically into a new current record, and only changes to the same field go to a coordinator. Lists are merged by element, so two workers adding different species, or one fixing an observer name while another adds an observer, both succeed without review. Original submissions are kept alongside the combined record, and each field of the combined record carries the submission it came from.
+
+Demonstration: the same two edits arrive. The server produces a current visit with A's corrected observer name and B's added species, with no review work. If instead both had changed the cover estimate of the same species, that one field would go to review.
+
+- Coherence — **partial.** Disjoint-field combination is correct for genuinely unrelated edits, which are the common case. It is wrong when the fields are related in a way the rules do not encode: A changes a species identification, B changes that entry's cover estimate, and the combined record pairs A's species with a cover B estimated for a different plant. Nothing flags it. Rules can encode known relationships (treat a species entry as one unit), but only the ones someone thought of.
+- Review workload — **strong.** Only same-field overlaps reach a coordinator.
+- Export readiness — **strong.** Visits with compatible edits are current immediately and exportable that week.
+- Upkeep — **weak.** Rules are needed for scalar fields, the observer list, and species entries, including how list elements are matched across edits, and they must be revisited whenever the visit's fields or the team's understanding of related fields changes.
+- Explainability — **partial.** Field-level provenance answers where each value came from, but the visit as a whole was authored by no one, and explaining it means explaining the rules.
+- Cost of changing later — **partial.** Falling back to whole-visit review is possible because originals are kept, but combined records already exported would need re-review to know whether any crossed a relationship.
+- Tablet storage — **partial.** The tablet keeps a base snapshot per edited visit and must produce structured changes correctly.
+
+Summary: trades a class of undetectable errors and ongoing rule upkeep for a much smaller review queue.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Whole-visit revisions.**
+
+The two major criteria point in opposite directions, so the selection turns on which failure the team would rather live with. A large review queue is a visible cost: unresolved visits are counted, listed, and withheld, and the team can see every week exactly what the policy is costing. A merge that pairs related edits wrongly is an invisible cost: the visit looks current, enters the CSV, and reaches the partner with nothing marking it for a second look. Under the tenet *Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable* and the non-goal of inferring scientific intent, the visible cost is the acceptable one. The upkeep criterion points the same way: a team with two coordinators and no systems administrator has no natural owner for merge rules.
+
+This is taken with the workload cost acknowledged, not denied. It works against the goal *Make reviewable problems visible before the Friday export* to the extent that the queue outgrows what two coordinators can clear in a week.
+
+### Why field-aware merge stays a live alternative
+
+Merge is the better policy if most competing revisions turn out to be edits to unrelated fields and the review queue regularly holds visits out of the Friday CSV. Neither is known before a season of use. The visit history records every competing pair with its base, so the share of review work a disjoint-field merge would have removed can be measured directly from stored data. Revisit this decision if:
+
+- unresolved visits routinely miss the Friday export because coordinators cannot clear them, and
+- a large share of those are pairs whose edits touch different fields.
+
+Because complete revisions and bases are retained, the move would not require new data from tablets for past visits. The intermediate step is cheaper than a full switch: compute a three-way merge as a *proposal* shown to the coordinator, who still approves it, which keeps a person on every combined visit while cutting the time each one takes. Encoding related fields as units (a species entry as one field) would narrow, not close, the coherence gap that decides against automatic merge here.
+
+### What this obligates downstream
+
+**Tablet-sync**
+
+- Keeps, for each locally held visit, the ID of the revision the local copy was edited from, and sends it as the base of the next edit. It keeps no base snapshot and computes no structured changes.
+- Generates the revision ID for each edit and keeps it stable across retries and restarts; an edit resubmitted under a new ID competes with itself.
+- Submits a chain of offline edits to one visit oldest first, since each names the previous as its base.
+- Sends the complete visit on every edit, however small the change.
+- Tells the worker when their edit was accepted as a competing revision, not as the current one, and shows that a visit is unresolved and what the competing revisions are. A worker should not conclude their correction is in effect because it was accepted.
+- Handles a visit whose state changed while the tablet was offline, including one a coordinator resolved: an edit made from a revision that is no longer current is still accepted and reopens the visit.
+
+**Review-export**
+
+- Presents each unresolved visit as a comparison of complete competing revisions against the revision they diverged from. Differences are computed for display only; the unit a coordinator decides on is the revision.
+- Expects review volume to include edits to unrelated fields, and is designed so those are fast to clear. This is where the policy's cost lands.
+- Holds every unresolved visit out of the CSV, and shows coordinators before Friday which visits are being withheld and why.
+- Needs no field-level provenance. An exported visit traces to one revision and, where there was one, a resolution record.
+- Accounts for a visit that was exported and later became unresolved when a late edit arrived.
